+## 16. Round 5 (NebDoors): the opposite door is placed with every Door opening
+Study + evidence: `work/looks/deliver3/doors_notes.md`.  **Game fact** [obs + disasm `$39df2`, `$3a12c`..`$3a2f6`]: a frog pressing Up on a door cell (`$83`) walks through the tower and comes out exactly half the ring away (x + 64 of 128 = column + 8 mod 16) on the SAME row, whether or not there is a door (or even a floor) there; the game never reads the destination cell before moving the frog.  The editor therefore keeps every `$83` cell paired: **placing a Door opening (menu item or D) also places a door on the same 3 rows in column ^ 8 (= (column + 8) mod 16); clearing or overwriting one door cell clears its partner cell (only if that is a door); the status line says "Door + opposite"**.  The Exit door (`$c3`) is the finish, is not paired, and says "Exit (finish)".  Rules in `place` (CODE1): old/new value `$83` -> partner `$83` / `0`; every other write path (enemies, lift stop, Empty, Ledge..., Exit) goes through the same routine; Copy tower, Blank grid and the lift code write cells directly and are not paired (originals are paired anyway, 176 of 178 door runs; the other two have the exit door opposite).  An overwrite of a non-door partner cell by a new door replaces it (a ledge, a lift cell); clearing a door whose opposite is NOT a door (old/foreign files) touches nothing there.  Format and old files: unchanged (version 1; unpaired doors load, play and edit as before).
+**Bytes**: `place` 38 -> 76 B (+38), `a_tri` message +16 B, strings `Door + opposite` / `Exit (finish)` 30 B (DATA); paid for by `cur_cell` and two copies of the column-base code in `place_enemy` now using `colbase` (-42 B).  `gen_ed.py`: CODE1 5312 (free 0), DATA 1324 (free 0), CODE2 1776 (16), CODE3 570 (6), CODE4 252 (4), CODE5 136 (2), CODE6 34 (4): total 9404 B, 32 B free.  `a_ekey` keeps its kind in d4 (`cur_cell` now trashes d2).  dadprg: `Hub::Ncmp::Doors` (pairing report, warning `unpaired_door` in the cart warnings and the level JSON `door_runs`/`door_warnings`), `LevelMap.vue` draws each door as one dark arched opening with a dashed tunnel line to its opposite.
